Z||000002||Chris McDonald||06-19-2001||11:22 AM||||Any qualified jobber a/c shop can make them if they are not available through your local auto parts store.<P>If you have nothing, at least get the lines from a junk yard and take them to an a/c shop to have new flexible lines pressed onto the metal tips.  <P>It should cost about $50 to $100 to have fresh lines made.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Cheever2||06-19-2001||12:32 PM||||If you mean the pair that goes across the firewall and has the heat-resistant material around part of it, and the fist-size bulb at one end...<P>I've got one.  $30 plus shipping.<P><P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/BUMP.gif"> ||||reg||6
Z||000004||V8Dreamer||06-19-2001||12:56 PM||||Sorry about that, it's an 86 2m4....thanks again,<P>Ryan||||reg||1
Z||000005||theogre||06-19-2001||07:46 PM||||If the aluminum hardware is ok then you can get new rubber put on it. The good thing there is the new hose will be "barrier type" which is much more resistant to R134.<P> ||||reg||1
Z||000006||FieroLT1||06-20-2001||08:02 AM||||Had mine redone for my LT1 swap and were also stretched 5" using the original hardware. New hoses are R134a GoodYear High Barrier. The whole thing was done in a couple hours by a competent A/C shop for $35 and that included new R134a O-rings. Not bad!<P><P>------------------<BR>Winston Ojeda<BR><B>88 Formula LT1</B><BR>Milwaukee, Wisconsin, USA<BR><A HREF="http://www.FieroLT1.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.FieroLT1.com</A><BR><I>Home of the Fiero LT1</I><BR>  <A HREF="mailto:wojeda@FieroLT1.com">wojeda@FieroLT1.com</A> ||||reg||1
